<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Patria Vieja</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 CE – 1814 CE · capital Santiago</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chile's first period of self-government, from the junta of 1810 to the royalist victory at Rancagua in 1814.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 18 September 1810, a date still celebrated as Chile's national day, a cabildo in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> created a governing junta led by the elderly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mateo-de-toro-zambrano">Mateo de Toro Zambrano</a>, ruling in the name of the captive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-vii-of-spain">Ferdinand VII</a>. It replaced the governor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-chile">Captaincy General of Chile</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The junta opened ports to free trade and convened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-national-congress-of-chile">First National Congress</a> in 1811. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a> seized power in a series of coups and introduced a provisional constitution, the first national flag and the newspaper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurora-de-chile">Aurora de Chile</a>, edited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camilo-henriquez">Camilo Henríquez</a>. Divisions between the Carreras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a> weakened the patriots as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-fernando-de-abascal">José Fernando de Abascal</a>, sent troops in 1813.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lircay">Treaty of Lircay</a> in 1814 briefly halted the war. Royalist forces then defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">O'Higgins</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rancagua">Rancagua</a> in October 1814, and the patriots fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendoza-argentina">Mendoza</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chacabuco">Chacabuco</a> in 1817.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/patria-vieja.jpg" alt="Patria Vieja" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chile_(1812-1814).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1810 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santiago</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mateo de Toro Zambrano, José Miguel Carrera, Bernardo O'Higgins</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Old Fatherland, First Chilean Junta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=patria-vieja" aria-label="Patria Vieja on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2875 3391 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Patria Vieja lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3195 3647L3195 3647M3195 3647L4910 989M3195 3647L3195 3647"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3195 3647h0M4910 989h0M3195 3647h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3195 3647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3195 3647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=patria-vieja">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
